lished yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Virginia sits slightly on the Democratic side of the partisan map, with a partisan lean of about three points toward Democrats, and the 2028 race is fully open: a term-limited incumbent president leaves both nominations contested. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or betting-market prices are available yet, and neither party has a nominee, so the outlook rests almost entirely on the state's underlying lean.\n\nRecent attention has centered on primary mechanics rather than the general election. Democrats have approved a calendar putting South Carolina first, and Virginia is weighing an early leap-day primary slot that would raise its voice in picking nominees. Headlines about the Democratic field's internal debates, including divisions over policy positions, describe nomination politics rather than general-election strength. Until nominees emerge and Virginia polling appears, the state remains a genuine tossup leaning modestly Democratic.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6351, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6229, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6055, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6063, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6125, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6095, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6104, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.6128, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.6085, "margin": 3.0}]}